---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp-lua/img/bp-snake-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Grow = `table.insert(segX, 1, newX)
table.insert(segY, 1, newY)

if newX == foodX and newY == foodY then
    foodX = math.random(1, COLS)
    foodY = math.random(1, ROWS)
else
    table.remove(segX)
    table.remove(segY)
end`;

const Turn = `if key == "up" and dirY == 0 then
    dirX = 0
    dirY = -1
end`;

const SelfHit = `for i = 1, #segX do
    if segX[i] == newX and segY[i] == newY then
        -- you ate yourself
    end
end`;

const Draw = `love.graphics.rectangle(
    "fill",
    (segX[i] - 1) * CELL + 1,
    (segY[i] - 1) * CELL + 1,
    CELL - 2,
    CELL - 2
)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Bonus Project: Snake</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The game that came on every phone before
            phones were interesting.</b> You steer a line around a grid, eating
            things. Every time you eat, you get longer, and the thing most
            likely to kill you becomes yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This is extra credit and it is optional.</b> Nothing later in the
            course depends on it. It exists because you finished early, or
            because you want to build something you can show somebody.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="before">
          <h2>Before You Start</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">You need everything through session 20.</b>{" "}
            Tables, going through a table, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="math.random" lang="lua" inline />.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Two things here are new</b>, and they are the two that make the
            whole game work:
          </p>

          <div class="text-start">
            <dl>
              <dt><CodeSnippet code="table.insert(t, 1, value)" lang="lua" inline /></dt>
              <dd>Puts a value at the <b>front</b> of the table and shifts everything else along. This is the exam's <CodeSnippet code="INSERT" lang="txt" inline />, and it is how the snake grows a new head.</dd>
              <dt><CodeSnippet code="table.remove(t)" lang="lua" inline /></dt>
              <dd>Takes the <b>last</b> value off. This is the exam's <CodeSnippet code="REMOVE" lang="txt" inline />, and it is how the tail catches up.</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">A snake is those two calls and nothing
            else.</b> Add a head, drop the tail, and the whole body appears to
            move without a single segment being told to.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-game" lang="txt" inline /> and
            rename it <CodeSnippet code="bp-snake" lang="txt" inline />. No new
            files, no libraries, nothing to install.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Build It</h2>

          <p>
            Five stages. <b class="text-sn-theme">Run it after every one.</b> A
            stage that works is worth more than three stages you cannot test.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">1. Think in cells, not pixels</h3>

          <p>
            Everything in this game lives on a grid. 40 columns, 30 rows, 20
            pixels each. <b>The snake's position is a column and a row</b>, and
            you only turn that into pixels when you draw:
          </p>

          <Code code={Draw} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            The <CodeSnippet code="- 1" lang="lua" inline /> is there because
            column 1 draws at pixel 0, and the{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="+ 1" lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="- 2" lang="lua" inline /> leave a one-pixel gap
            so you can see the segments.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Start with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code={`segX = {5, 4, 3}`} lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code={`segY = {15, 15, 15}`} lang="lua" inline />, and
            a loop that draws them. Three squares in a row. Nothing moves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">2. A clock, and a direction</h3>

          <p>
            A snake does not slide, it steps. Use the same timer idea as any
            turn-based thing: add <CodeSnippet code="dt" lang="lua" inline /> to
            a total, and when it passes{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="0.12" lang="lua" inline />, take one step and
            reset it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two variables, <CodeSnippet code="dirX" lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="dirY" lang="lua" inline />, hold which way it is
            going &mdash; one of them is 1 or &minus;1 and the other is 0. The
            new head is the old head plus the direction.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">3. Move by growing and shrinking</h3>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">This is the whole game and it is nine
            lines:</b>
          </p>

          <Code code={Grow} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            Always add a head. <b>Only drop the tail if you did not eat.</b>{" "}
            That single <CodeSnippet code="else" lang="lua" inline /> is the
            difference between moving and growing, and there is no other
            growth code anywhere in the program.
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>Draw the food before this works.</b> One square, a different
            color, at a random cell. Then eat it and watch the snake get one
            longer. If it gets longer every step, your{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="else" lang="lua" inline /> is in the wrong place.
          </div>

          <h3 class="mt-4">4. Steering, and the reverse bug</h3>

          <Code code={Turn} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="and dirY == 0" lang="lua" inline /> is not
            tidiness.</b> Without it, a snake travelling down can be told to go
            up, which walks its head straight into its own neck and kills it
            instantly. Leave the check out once and try it &mdash; it is the
            fastest death in the project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">5. Dying</h3>

          <p>Two ways, and they are different kinds of check:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>The wall.</b> Four comparisons on the new head's column and
              row.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Yourself.</b> A loop over every segment, asking whether the new
              head lands on it:
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={SelfHit} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            Both send everything back to the starting three squares.{" "}
            <b>Do that check before you insert the new head</b>, or the head you
            are testing against is already in the table and every step is a
            collision.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>What Success Looks Like</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image
              src={TargetImage}
              alt="A black 800 by 600 window with a green snake eight segments long bending from horizontal to vertical, and a red food square to its right"
              width={800}
              class="img-fluid rounded"
            />
          </div>

          <p>
            Eight segments after five meals, mid-turn.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Notice the corner.</b> Nothing in the code
            knows about corners &mdash; the bend is what happens when you add a
            head in a new direction and keep dropping the tail.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="extend">
          <h2>Then Make It Yours</h2>

          <ul>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Score, and speed.</b> Count the meals and shorten the step time
              as it goes up. A snake that gets faster is a much better game than
              one that only gets longer.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Do not put food inside the snake.</b> Right now it can appear
              under your own body, which is unwinnable and looks like a bug
              because it is one. Keep picking until the cell is empty.{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">This is the most valuable fix on the
              list.</b>
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>A head that looks like a head.</b> Draw segment 1 in a
              different color. One <CodeSnippet code="if" lang="lua" inline />{" "}
              inside the drawing loop.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Wrap instead of dying at the wall.</b> Leave the right edge,
              come back on the left. Then decide which version you would rather
              play, and say why.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Buffer the turn.</b> Press two directions faster than one step
              and the first one is lost. Remember the next direction instead of
              applying it immediately. This is what makes commercial versions
              feel responsive, and it is one extra variable.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Submit whatever you have.</b> A snake that moves and eats still
            earns credit, and so does a finished game with a bug you can
            describe.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Project Complete
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
